title The floor that stopped biting the hour the work it invited arrived
prompt Copal's second round -- the baton, with the prior lap's recommend naming bron-resins as the other half of the seat.
think Council rota row 1 of 5, lap N 3996 -- Air, the row that feels: law and boundary, and its test is tactile. Press each edge; if the hand passes through, the boundary was a wish. The fixed seat is sharper still -- you check whether a part stands free by trying to pull it out. That test picked this lap.
think Opened with a cold roster pass and held still while it ran: 124 guards, 888s, 119 green, 1 red, 4 gated. The one red is agent_jail, already booked as %446 and an environment fact on a door where nothing is broken -- not mine and not new.
think The prior lap's recommend pointed at bron-resins/, so I pulled on it first. It holds no Rye at all: 16 .bron resins, a manifest, one handoff, and three census scans that exclude it by name as testimony. Three living tools name bron-resins/pond-supersede-exit.bron, which is ABSENT -- and the absence is the design: the scorecard asserts that file absent while the season sleeps. The boundary held. No fault, and saying so is the honest end of that thread.
think Pulled the next part: amphora/tally_copy.rye. A symlink, mode 120000, to the one law -- and all 23 of its siblings across the tree are symlinks too, not one copy among them. Single strand, tree-wide, measured rather than assumed.
think The lap arrived from the roster's own comments. TWO rostered guards name amphora/vessel_fetch_wire.rye as Amphora's room to tie, both written so a later tie walks free rather than reds. That is a boundary the whole tree agreed existed and nothing under the hand held.
think Measured before writing a line, which is what the mantra tie beside it did. Carriage: max_resin_bytes <= max_resin_chunks * max_chunk_body, 1024 <= 8 * 267 = 2136. Raising the whole to 2200 in a throwaway pen and changing nothing else refuses 64 of the 2,200 legal lengths with Overflow -- and the module's own selftest still reads GREEN, because its chunk fixture is 400 bytes. The fault ships green, which is the whole argument for a tie.
think Second relation, which no comment anywhere named: the assembler dedupes chunks with one u16 bit per index, so an index must fit the mask. Raised max_resin_chunks to 20 with the carriage relation still holding and it panics at chunk index 16 on a well-formed resin -- a crash rather than a refusal, since the shift amount stops fitting the mask's log2 type. Named the width ChunkMask so the assert has something to point at rather than a second bare literal.
think Proved the ties bite before landing them: as written the module builds and selftests GREEN, and each violation stops the build at its own assert by name. A tie proven only in the passing direction cannot be told from a comment.
think Then the round turned. comlink_carriage_witness had been GREEN in the cold pass forty minutes earlier and reddened at its control on the first run after my tie. Root, not symptom: its gate reads ties_wired -ge 2 over three rooms. With two tied, one dropping its tie read 1 and refused, so the floor LOOKED like single-drop detection. My third tie took the count to 3, a single drop leaves 2, and 2 clears a floor of 2. Control cases 16 and 17 turned from REFUSES to WRONG in the same hour.
think The tempting repair was to mark 16 and 17 as ok, which would have been fixing the test to match a weakened gate. The honest one strengthens the gate: ask each room for its OWN tie rather than for any of the three, and want all three -- which is the shape capacity_holds -eq 3 ten lines above had the whole time. Control back to 20 cases, 17 refusals, 3 welcomes, verdict proven, with all three single drops refusing.
think Case 19 turned over rather than passing: it used to plant the Amphora tie and expect ok, which proves nothing once the real file carries it. It is now Amphora dropping its own tie, and refuses.
think Checked the sibling for the same shape rather than assuming it was singular. comlink_glow_tend gates ties_wired -lt 2 over literal_rooms_found=3 and reads 2 today -- sitting exactly on its floor, still biting a single drop, with comlink/vessel_fetch_wire.rye holding the max_wire_payload literal and no tie. It goes quiet the day comlink ties its third room. That is comlink's lane and its guard is green, so it is named in the row rather than repaired across the boundary -- and named BEFORE it fires.
obs A guard's floor is a claim about a minimum; a regression test is a claim about a change. They read identically while the population sits on the floor, and they part the moment it rises. What makes this one sharp is that the rise was the work the guard's own comment invited by name, and whose exact spelling it already carried a grep for.
obs The comment reasoned carefully about the opposite risk -- a gate spelled = 2 would red on their honest work the day they take it -- and got that right. It guarded the third tie from punishment and left the floor to stop biting when it landed. Care aimed at one failure, with its back to the other.
obs Two of the four card readings I ran were mine to set, and I set service to 100 first out of habit. The tool prints named_by_card=no, so the honest score is three of four questions: 75, which reads A/94 rather than A+/100. A number I choose is not a measurement.
obs The card had 105 bytes of headroom and needed about 600. It was carrying the agent_jail account twice -- as booked row %446 and as an elder paragraph from before that row existed -- so the merge freed the room rather than a fold, and the cairn says so.
obs Closing yesterday's shelf surfaced a smaller drift beside it. session-logs/CHAPTERS.md read 20 for 20260831 where the shelf holds 26, and 64 for 20260903 where it holds 65, had never closed 20260904, and did not list 20260905 at all -- while session-logs/README.md agreed with the measurement on every one. Two living pages describing one set of facts, and only one of them was being derived. Repaired from the count, which is the page's own stated rule.
obs The rebase taught the derived-number lesson twice more. Closing 20260905 in the pin, a peer wrote 56 and I measured 57; after the rebase brought their logs in, the shelf holds 58, with logs, index rows and distinct stamps all agreeing. Neither earlier number was careless -- both were read against a tree that was not the one shipping. The pin's own rule already says a day's number is derived on the lap that closes it, and that rule only holds if the count is taken after the last merge rather than before the first.
obs The %440 lantern fired a fourth time: the clean auto-merge of today's day shelf seated my row BELOW an older one, so newest-first was lost with no conflict to notice. Sorted again. Four firings across two days is a loom's worth of evidence, and the loom is already a booked lap rather than mine to open here.
obs A peer condensed the same duplicate agent_jail paragraph I did, in the same hour, to a pointer line where I merged its one unique clause into the %446 row. Two hands tidying one thing independently is the shape %291 names; the conflict resolved to the tighter of the two, since the card was at its ceiling and the row already carried the clause.
obs THE ROW YIELDED ITS NUMBER, by the law rather than by a judgment. Booked %453 from a fetched --next read; the first rebase left it free, and the second found a peer had published a %449 at stamp 20260906.001558 on the shelf REDS-three-guards-that-read-a-proxy-rows-447-449.md. Theirs is shared and frozen, mine was unshared, so mine moved to %453 across four files while its stamp 20260906.010047 stayed the key through both numbers. Two peers' logs in this same day shelf record the identical shape, one of them four times in one send.
obs The second rebase also brought a peer raising the card bound 32,768 -> 40,960, after I had folded a landed account to fit under the old one. The fold stands on its own reasoning rather than on the ceiling, and the cairn now says so instead of claiming a necessity that stopped being true an hour later.
obs MY FOLD WAS A DUPLICATE AND WAS WITHDRAWN, the same shape as last lap's %438 shelf. Patchouli had shelved Petrichor's mark-law weave to archive/20260906-001820 an hour before I shelved it to 011500; theirs published first, mine is deleted, and the card points at the one that stands. Three duplicate folds in two days across this fleet, all resolved the same way by hand. The ledger's NUMBERING has an allocator now and its FOLDING does not, which is %291's shape one room over and is where the next instrument belongs.
obs THE ROW WORE THREE NUMBERS: %449 booked from --next, yielded to a peer's published %449 (stamp 20260906.001558), moved to %452, yielded again to a peer's published %452 (stamp 20260906.002658), and landed %453. Both peers' stamps were EARLIER than my 20260906.010047 and both were shared, so the law decided both moves and no judgment entered either.
obs THE AMEND WAS REFUSED BY A PIN BOUND, which is the gate working. construction/REDS.md stood at 26,437 against 24,576 once my row landed beside three peers' from the same two hours, so the commit could not close until the pin fit. Folded my OWN row -- CLOSED, complete, and the only one whose folding costs no peer anything -- through tools/fixtures/r/reds_fold.sh, which refused my first shelf name for lacking 'rows-' in the basename and then moved the row, re-anchored its links and wrote the recital line itself. 23,597 of 24,576.
file construction/archive/REDS-the-floor-that-stopped-biting-rows-453-453.md the row folded whole so the pin keeps what is OPEN
